{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "Yes -- Detroit and Chicago had a direct rail connection well before 1883: the Michigan Central Railroad, completed May 21, 1852 (the Michigan Southern, a separate competing company, had reached Chicago via a more southerly route through Ohio and Indiana three months earlier, in February 1852, but that's a different line, not depicted here). By 1883 this was a well-established, mature main line, already drifting into Vanderbilt/New York Central's orbit. Notably, unlike several other historical lines on this map (the Big Four's Cleveland-Cincinnati route, the South Carolina Railroad), this corridor was NOT abandoned -- it survives essentially intact as Amtrak's modern Wolverine service (see amtrak.geojson), making it one of the few lines on this map with an unbroken 170+ year passenger-rail history. Geometry is real-traced from OpenStreetMap active-rail geometry for nearly the entire route (Detroit-Jackson-Battle Creek-Kalamazoo-New Buffalo-Porter IN-Kensington), reflecting that continuity. Only the final ~15km approach into downtown Chicago (dense terminal trackage) could not be matched to a connected path and is a straight-line approximation.", "sources": ["https://www.american-rails.com/mc.html (Detroit-Chicago main line completed 1852, 285 miles via Jackson-Kalamazoo-New Buffalo, then Illinois Central trackage rights via Kensington into Chicago; Vanderbilt acquired the connecting Canada Southern Jan 1 1876, full Michigan Central integration completed 1878; NYC controlled 94% of MC stock by 1916; Penn Central merger Dec 1970; Conrail 1976; Amtrak still operates the former MC main line between Porter, Indiana and Kalamazoo today)", "https://kids.kiddle.co/History_of_railroads_in_Michigan (first Michigan Central train Detroit-Chicago ran May 21 1852; Michigan Southern had reached Chicago via a different, more southerly route in February 1852, three months earlier)"], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact frequency/timetable for the 1852-1900 era; precise date NYC formally absorbed Michigan Central as a subsidiary (vs. just majority stock control); named passenger trains before the 20th-century Mercury/Wolverine era.", "geometry_sources": ["OpenStreetMap (via Overpass API), railway=rail ways tagged usage=main, for the Detroit-Jackson-Battle Creek-Kalamazoo-New Buffalo-Porter-Kensington corridor -- still active track today as Amtrak's Wolverine route"], "chicago_terminal_note": "Chicago end truncated Aug 2026. The trace previously ran past its terminal, doubled back south down the ex-Illinois Central main line to 71st Street, then jumped 9 km further south and 18 km back north to a downtown-Chicago anchor point that lay on no track at all. It now ends where the route reaches the ex-Illinois Central main at 18th Street -- the approach to Central Station, the Michigan Central's Chicago passenger terminal from 1893. Note the Chicago approach still contains straight-line chords of about 7.8 km and 5.0 km where the trace is incomplete, and follows the modern NS Chicago Line alignment rather than the historic Michigan Central/Illinois Central route in from Kensington.", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Michigan Central Railroad", "line": "Michigan Central: Detroit \u2013 Chicago (via Jackson and Kalamazoo)", "id": "michigan-central-detroit-chicago", "color": "#6A4C93", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 60, "speed_source": "estimated: mainline steam/diesel intercity service across the full 1852-1971 span; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1852, "end": 1878, "headway_min": 480, "note": "First train ran May 21 1852; independent-era Michigan Central, gradually falling under Vanderbilt influence starting with the 1876 Canada Southern acquisition, full integration by 1878; frequency estimated, unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://www.american-rails.com/mc.html", "https://kids.kiddle.co/History_of_railroads_in_Michigan"]}, {"start": 1878, "end": 1916, "headway_min": 300, "note": "Vanderbilt/New York Central affiliate era; NYC held 94% of MC stock by 1916; frequency estimated, unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://www.american-rails.com/mc.html", "https://kids.kiddle.co/History_of_railroads_in_Michigan"]}, {"start": 1916, "end": 1968, "headway_min": 180, "note": "NYC-controlled subsidiary; frequency estimated, unsourced. By November 1938 NYC's streamlined Mercury service ran this route via Jackson, Battle Creek, and Kalamazoo.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://www.american-rails.com/mc.html", "https://kids.kiddle.co/History_of_railroads_in_Michigan"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-86.00094, 42.09108], "to": [-86.24809, 41.83883], "chord_mi": 21.54, "traced_mi": 21.57, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Michigan Subdivision (20.5 mi)", "Michigan Subdivision (Niles Controlled Siding) (1.07 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-86.48689, 41.80225], "to": [-86.69891, 41.80011], "chord_mi": 10.86, "traced_mi": 10.93, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Michigan Subdivision (10.93 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-83.27167, 42.2977], "to": [-83.59527, 42.24185], "chord_mi": 17.02, "traced_mi": 17.0, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Michigan Subdivision (17.0 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}], "geometry_quality": "Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 1 ruled segment totalling 22 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed). Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 3 ruled segments totalling 49 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed)."}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-83.0458, 42.3314], [-83.07001, 42.32474], [-83.07642, 42.32711], [-83.07941, 42.32781], [-83.08364, 42.32828], [-83.08674, 42.32813], [-83.12241, 42.32315], [-83.12918, 42.32281], [-83.13626, 42.32112], [-83.23496, 42.30681], [-83.24743, 42.30396], [-83.2716711, 42.2977043], [-83.2752788, 42.2967818], [-83.277053, 42.296409], [-83.314759, 42.290118], [-83.323034, 42.288671], [-83.3583613, 42.2827526], [-83.475068, 42.262994], [-83.5442223, 42.2515068], [-83.5749211, 42.2463479], [-83.5771661, 42.245921], [-83.5791536, 42.2454405], [-83.5885358, 42.2428195], [-83.589652, 42.242545], [-83.5908991, 42.2422852], [-83.5923048, 42.2420604], [-83.5937512, 42.2419115], [-83.5952697, 42.2418525], [-83.60215, 42.24225], [-83.60358, 42.24253], [-83.60529, 42.24322], [-83.60812, 42.24535], [-83.61219, 42.24